.TH SEMVER-FORGE 1 "2026-11-03" "semver-forge 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
semver-forge \- automatic semantic version tracking and synchronization
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B semver-forge
.RI [ --project DIR ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B semver-forge
provides five project commands: init, next, sync, config, and check. It initializes version tracking, determines the next Semantic Versioning increment from Git history, applies that version to configured files, and synchronizes metadata.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init
Inspect the project and create .semver-forge.toml. Existing version values are preserved. Conflicting recognized version sources must be resolved before initialization. Use --yes to authorize replacing an existing configuration.
.TP
.B next
Inspect commits since the last processed Git boundary. Select the highest configured impact (breaking change, feature, or fix/performance), update the canonical version source and configured targets when a change is warranted, and persist the processed commit boundary. Re-running next does not count the same commits twice.
.TP
.B sync
Synchronize configured version metadata to the canonical version. Use --dry-run to preview. Use --version VERSION to supply a different target version. Use --discover to recursively find supported metadata files, persist new targets to project configuration, and synchronize them.
.TP
.B config
View or manage system, user, and project configuration. Use --get KEY, --set KEY TOML_VALUE, or --unset KEY. The default write scope is project; --global selects ~/.svfconfig and --system selects /etc/svfconfig or $PREFIX/etc/svfconfig in Termux. --edit opens the selected file.
.TP
.B check
Report canonical version, synchronization status, discovered metadata, and configuration scopes without writing or synchronizing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --project DIR
Operate on the specified project directory. Defaults to the current directory.
.TP
.B --json
Emit structured JSON output.
.TP
.B --dry-run
For next or sync, preview changes without writing.
.TP
.B --explain
For next, include the reason for the selected version or no-change decision.
.TP
.B --discover
For sync, recursively discover supported version metadata, persist newly discovered targets in project configuration, then synchronize. With --dry-run neither configuration nor metadata is written.
.TP
.B --global
For config, select the user configuration file ~/.svfconfig.
.TP
.B --system
For config, select /etc/svfconfig or $PREFIX/etc/svfconfig in Termux.
.TP
.B --edit
For config, open the selected scope file with VISUAL, EDITOR, or vi.
.TP
.B --yes
For init, authorize replacing existing project configuration.
.TP
.B --version
Print the installed SemVer Forge version.
.SH VERSION SELECTION
Conventional Commit types are classified by the project's .semver-forge.toml configuration. By default, breaking changes select a major increment, feat selects minor, and fix or perf selects patch. Documentation, tests, formatting, refactoring, build, CI, and chore commits are ignored by default. The highest qualifying impact wins.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .semver-forge.toml
Project configuration, including the canonical version source, synchronization targets, commit rules, and processed Git history boundary.
.TP
.I ~/.svfconfig
User-level configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/svfconfig
System-level configuration; in Termux use $PREFIX/etc/svfconfig.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
semver-forge init
semver-forge next
semver-forge next --dry-run --explain
semver-forge sync --dry-run
semver-forge sync --discover --dry-run
semver-forge config --get commits.types.feat
semver-forge check
semver-forge sync
.fi
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Git is required for next. Python 3.10 or later is required.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR python3 (1)
